```

`cd->offset` is the absolute position of the central directory. `buf_offset` is the offset of the buffer in the source. If `cd->offset >= buf_offset`, the central directory is within the buffer. `cd->offset - buf_offset` is the offset within the buffer. `cd->size` is the size of the central directory.

If `cd->offset + cd->size` extends past the buffer, `_zip_buffer_get` returns NULL. This is safe.

But what if `cd->offset < buf_offset`? Then the code seeks to `cd->offset` and reads the entries directly from the source. The entries are read one at a time, and each read is bounds-checked. This is also safe.

Wait, but what if `cd->offset` is very close to `buf_offset`? For example, if `cd->offset = buf_offset - 1` and `cd->size = 100`, then the central directory starts just before the buffer and extends into it. The code seeks to `buf_offset - 1` and reads the entries from the source. The first entry might be partially in the source and partially in the buffer. But `_zip_dirent_read` reads from the source (since `cd_buffer` is NULL), so it reads the entire entry from the source. This is safe.

